{"name":"Blue Dolphin","hex":"#bcc5cf","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"dolphin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A carnivorous cetacean, typically with a beak-like snout, famed for their intelligence and occasional willingness to approach humans.","Tursiops truncatus, (Atlantic bottlenose dolphin) the most well-known species."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A man-made semi submerged maritime structure, usually installed to provide a fixed structure for temporary mooring, to prevent ships from drifting to shallow water or to serve as base for navigational aids."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dolphin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Blue Dolphin","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Dolphin was an auxiliary schooner built in 1926 at Shelburne, Nova Scotia by the Shelburne Shipbuilding Company as an adventure yacht. She served as a United States Navy auxiliary and given the hull number IX-65 in World War II. The vessel then served as a postwar research vessel made famous by a Stan Rogers song."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Blue_Dolphin_at_Sail.jpg/330px-Blue_Dolphin_at_Sail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Dolphin","wikidata":"Q4929067"},{"title":"Blue Dolphin Energy Company","blocks":[{"text":"Blue Dolphin Energy Company is a publicly traded Delaware corporation, headquartered in Houston, primarily engaged in the refining and marketing of petroleum products to be used as jet fuel, or as \"a light sweet crude.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Blue_Dolphin_Energy_Wilson_County_Texas_2025.jpg/330px-Blue_Dolphin_Energy_Wilson_County_Texas_2025.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue_Dolphin_Energy_Company","wikidata":"Q4929068"},{"title":"Dolphin","blocks":[{"text":"A dolphin is any one of the 40 extant species of aquatic mammal from the cetacean families Delphinidae, Platanistidae, Iniidae, Pontoporiidae, and the probably extinct Lipotidae. All these families belong to the parvorder Odontoceti, i.e., toothed whales, which also include the closely related families Monodontidae and Phocoenidae (porpoises), as well as the more distant families Physeteroidea and Ziphiidae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Tursiops_truncatus_01.jpg/330px-Tursiops_truncatus_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolphin","wikidata":"Q7369"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Blue_Dolphin_in_port.jpg/500px-Blue_Dolphin_in_port.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Blue_Dolphin_in_port.jpg/1280px-Blue_Dolphin_in_port.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Blue_Dolphin_in_port.jpg/500px-Blue_Dolphin_in_port.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Blue Dolphin in port","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_Dolphin_in_port.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Blue_Dolphin_wreck%2C_August_26_2021.jpg/500px-Blue_Dolphin_wreck%2C_August_26_2021.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Blue_Dolphin_wreck%2C_August_26_2021.jpg/1280px-Blue_Dolphin_wreck%2C_August_26_2021.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Blue_Dolphin_wreck%2C_August_26_2021.jpg/500px-Blue_Dolphin_wreck%2C_August_26_2021.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The sunken Blue Dolphin at Detroit, August 2021","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlue_Dolphin_wreck%2C_August_26_2021.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Delfinekko.gif/330px-Delfinekko.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Delfinekko.gif/330px-Delfinekko.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Biosonar by cetaceans","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADelfinekko.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedi